\pard\sa60\sb0\keepn\fs18\cf3 Question 1  \u183?  Infection and response  \u183?  cs-bio-vaccination-6\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\keepn\fs24\b Explain how a vaccine protects a person from becoming ill with a disease, and explain why a person who has been vaccinated does not usually become ill if they later meet the pathogen. [6 marks]\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs20 Marks: 6      Command word: Explain\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Level descriptors\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 3 (5-6 marks)}\line A clear, ordered account of both halves. The vaccine is described as containing a dead or inactive form of the pathogen, white blood cells (lymphocytes) are described as producing antibodies with a shape complementary to the antigen, and memory cells are named as what remains afterwards. The second exposure is then explained as a faster and larger antibody response that destroys the pathogen before symptoms appear.\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 2 (3-4 marks)}\line Both halves are attempted and some of the science is correct, but a link is missing. Typically antibodies are mentioned without antigens or without memory cells, or the second response is described as "the body remembers" without saying that antibodies are produced faster and in greater quantity.\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 1 (1-2 marks)}\line Isolated correct statements with no chain of reasoning. E.g. "a vaccine has a weak version of the germ in it" or "your body makes antibodies", with nothing joining the first exposure to the second.\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Indicative content\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  A vaccine contains a small quantity of dead or inactivated forms of the pathogen.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  The pathogen carries antigens on its surface.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  White blood cells (lymphocytes) respond by producing antibodies.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  An antibody has a shape complementary to a specific antigen, so it is specific to that pathogen.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  Memory cells remain in the body after the vaccination.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  On meeting the live pathogen, memory cells produce antibodies far more quickly and in much greater quantity (the secondary response).\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  The pathogen is destroyed before it can reproduce enough to cause symptoms, so the person does not become ill.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  If a large proportion of the population is vaccinated, the spread of the pathogen is much reduced (herd immunity).\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Marking points (as stored, for point-by-point marking)\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 1.  The vaccine contains a dead or inactivated form of the pathogen.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 2.  The pathogen carries antigens on its surface.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 3.  White blood cells / lymphocytes produce antibodies in response.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 4.  The antibody produced is specific \u8212? its shape is complementary to that antigen.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 5.  Memory cells remain in the body afterwards.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 6.  On a second exposure the antibodies are produced faster and in greater quantity.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 7.  The pathogen is destroyed before symptoms develop, so the person does not become ill.\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf3\sa160\sb120\fs4 \par
\pard\sa60\sb0\keepn\fs18\cf3 Question 2  \u183?  Homeostasis and response  \u183?  cs-bio-homeostasis-glucose-6\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\keepn\fs24\b A person eats a meal containing a large amount of carbohydrate. Explain how their body detects and corrects the change in blood glucose concentration that follows. [6 marks]\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs20 Marks: 6      Command word: Explain\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Level descriptors\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 3 (5-6 marks)}\line A complete negative feedback loop. The rise is detected by the pancreas, insulin is named as the hormone released, it travels in the blood, and its effect is given specifically \u8212? glucose moves from the blood into liver and muscle cells and is converted to glycogen for storage. The account closes the loop by saying blood glucose falls back towards normal and insulin release then decreases.\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 2 (3-4 marks)}\line The pancreas and insulin are correctly identified and an effect is given, but the mechanism is incomplete \u8212? for example "insulin lowers blood sugar" without saying glucose enters cells or is stored as glycogen, or the loop is never closed.\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 1 (1-2 marks)}\line Isolated correct facts, such as naming insulin or naming the pancreas, with no explanation of how the correction happens.\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Indicative content\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  Blood glucose concentration rises after the carbohydrate is digested and absorbed.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  The change is monitored and controlled by the pancreas.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  The pancreas releases the hormone insulin into the blood.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  Insulin travels in the blood to the liver and to muscle cells.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  Insulin causes glucose to move from the blood into these cells.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  In the liver and muscles, excess glucose is converted to glycogen and stored.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  Blood glucose concentration therefore falls back towards its normal level.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  This is negative feedback: as the level returns to normal, less insulin is released.\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Marking points (as stored, for point-by-point marking)\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 1.  Blood glucose concentration rises after the meal is digested and absorbed.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 2.  The pancreas monitors and controls blood glucose concentration.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 3.  The pancreas releases insulin into the blood.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 4.  Insulin causes glucose to move from the blood into liver and muscle cells.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 5.  Excess glucose is converted to glycogen and stored.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 6.  Blood glucose concentration falls back towards normal.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 7.  This is negative feedback \u8212? insulin release decreases as the level returns to normal.\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf3\sa160\sb120\fs4 \par
\pard\sa60\sb0\keepn\fs18\cf3 Question 3  \u183?  Organisation  \u183?  cs-bio-enzyme-temperature-4\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\keepn\fs24\b The rate of an enzyme-controlled reaction increases as the temperature rises to about 40 \u176?C, and then falls sharply above about 50 \u176?C. Explain why the rate changes in this way. [4 marks]\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs20 Marks: 4      Command word: Explain\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Mark scheme \u8212? one mark for each point\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 1.  As temperature increases, the enzyme and substrate molecules have more kinetic energy and move faster.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 2.  So there are more frequent successful collisions between enzyme and substrate, and the rate increases.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 3.  Above the optimum, the bonds holding the enzyme in shape break and the active site changes shape \u8212? the enzyme denatures.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 4.  The substrate no longer fits the active site, so no enzyme-substrate complexes form and the rate falls sharply.\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf3\sa160\sb120\fs4 \par
\pard\sa60\sb0\keepn\fs18\cf3 Question 4  \u183?  Organisation  \u183?  cs-bio-small-intestine-4\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\keepn\fs24\b Explain how the small intestine is adapted for the efficient absorption of the products of digestion. [4 marks]\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs20 Marks: 4      Command word: Explain\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Mark scheme \u8212? one mark for each point\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 1.  The lining is folded and covered in villi (and microvilli), which give a very large surface area for absorption.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 2.  The wall of each villus is one cell thick, giving a short diffusion pathway.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 3.  Each villus has a good blood supply, which carries absorbed molecules away and maintains a steep concentration gradient.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 4.  The small intestine is long, so food is in contact with the absorbing surface for a long time.  {\i (marked not essential)}\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf3\sa160\sb120\fs4 \par
\pard\sa60\sb0\keepn\fs18\cf3 Question 5  \u183?  Inheritance, variation and evolution  \u183?  cs-bio-antibiotic-resistance-4\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\keepn\fs24\b A hospital finds that an antibiotic which used to treat an infection no longer works. Explain how a population of bacteria becomes resistant to an antibiotic. [4 marks]\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs20 Marks: 4      Command word: Explain\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Mark scheme \u8212? one mark for each point\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 1.  A random mutation occurs in the DNA of an individual bacterium.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 2.  By chance, that mutation makes the bacterium resistant to the antibiotic.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 3.  When the antibiotic is used, the non-resistant bacteria are killed but the resistant one survives.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 4.  The survivor reproduces and passes the resistance on, so the resistant strain becomes common in the population.\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs10 \par
