\pard\sa160\sb120\keepn\fs28\cf2\b CHEMISTRY\par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf3\sa160\sb120\fs4 \par
\pard\sa60\sb0\keepn\fs18\cf3 Question 6  \u183?  The rate and extent of chemical change  \u183?  cs-chem-rates-collision-6\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\keepn\fs24\b Marble chips react with dilute hydrochloric acid. Explain, using collision theory, why the rate of this reaction increases when the concentration of the acid is increased and when the temperature is increased. [6 marks]\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs20 Marks: 6      Command word: Explain\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Level descriptors\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 3 (5-6 marks)}\line Both variables are explained separately and correctly, and both explanations run through collision theory to a rate. Concentration is explained as more particles in the same volume, so more frequent collisions. Temperature is explained as BOTH more frequent collisions and \u8212? the point that earns Level 3 \u8212? a greater proportion of collisions having energy above the activation energy.\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 2 (3-4 marks)}\line Both variables are addressed and collision theory is used, but one explanation is incomplete. Most often temperature is explained only as "particles move faster so collide more often", with no mention of activation energy; or concentration is described as "more acid" without saying more particles in a given volume.\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 1 (1-2 marks)}\line Simple correct statements about the rate increasing, or that particles collide more, without linking the change to the frequency or energy of collisions.\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Indicative content\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  A reaction happens only when particles collide with at least the activation energy \u8212? a successful collision.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  Increasing the concentration means more acid particles in the same volume of solution.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  So collisions between acid particles and the marble surface are more frequent.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  More frequent successful collisions per second means a faster rate.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  Increasing the temperature gives the particles more kinetic energy, so they move faster.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  Faster particles collide more frequently.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  A greater proportion of collisions now have energy greater than or equal to the activation energy.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  Both effects increase the number of successful collisions per second, so the rate increases \u8212? the energy effect is the larger of the two.\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Marking points (as stored, for point-by-point marking)\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 1.  A reaction occurs only when particles collide with at least the activation energy.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 2.  Higher concentration means more acid particles in the same volume.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 3.  So collisions with the marble are more frequent, and the rate increases.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 4.  Higher temperature gives particles more kinetic energy so they move faster.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 5.  Faster-moving particles collide more frequently.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 6.  A greater proportion of collisions have energy above the activation energy.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 7.  More successful collisions per second means a faster rate of reaction.\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf3\sa160\sb120\fs4 \par
\pard\sa60\sb0\keepn\fs18\cf3 Question 7  \u183?  Chemical changes  \u183?  cs-chem-electrolysis-molten-6\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\keepn\fs24\b Molten lead bromide is electrolysed using inert electrodes. Explain why the compound must be molten, and explain what is formed at each electrode and why. [6 marks]\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs20 Marks: 6      Command word: Explain\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Level descriptors\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 3 (5-6 marks)}\line A complete account. Melting is explained as freeing the ions to move and carry charge. Both electrodes are named correctly with the ion that moves to each, the process at each is identified as gain or loss of electrons, and both products are given: lead at the cathode, bromine at the anode. Reduction and oxidation are used correctly.\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 2 (3-4 marks)}\line Both products are given and the movement of ions is described, but the electron transfer is vague or one electrode is unexplained \u8212? for example "bromine is made at the positive electrode" without saying that bromide ions lose electrons.\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 1 (1-2 marks)}\line One correct product, or a correct statement that ions move to oppositely charged electrodes, with no further explanation.\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Indicative content\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  In the solid, the ions are held in fixed positions in the lattice and cannot move.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  Melting frees the ions so they are mobile and can carry charge to the electrodes.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  Positive lead ions are attracted to the negative electrode (cathode).\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  At the cathode each lead ion gains two electrons and is reduced to a lead atom, so molten lead forms.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  Negative bromide ions are attracted to the positive electrode (anode).\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  At the anode each bromide ion loses an electron and is oxidised; pairs of bromine atoms join, so bromine gas is formed.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  The overall change is that the compound is decomposed into its two elements.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  Inert electrodes are used so that the electrodes themselves do not take part in the reaction.\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Marking points (as stored, for point-by-point marking)\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 1.  In the solid the ions are fixed in the lattice and cannot move.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 2.  Melting frees the ions so they can move and carry charge.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 3.  Positive lead ions move to the negative electrode (cathode).\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 4.  At the cathode lead ions gain electrons (reduction) and lead is formed.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 5.  Negative bromide ions move to the positive electrode (anode).\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 6.  At the anode bromide ions lose electrons (oxidation) and bromine is formed.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 7.  The compound is decomposed into its elements.\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf3\sa160\sb120\fs4 \par
\pard\sa60\sb0\keepn\fs18\cf3 Question 8  \u183?  Bonding, structure and the properties of matter  \u183?  cs-chem-ionic-properties-4\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\keepn\fs24\b Explain, in terms of structure and bonding, why sodium chloride has a high melting point but does not conduct electricity when it is solid. [4 marks]\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs20 Marks: 4      Command word: Explain\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Mark scheme \u8212? one mark for each point\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 1.  Sodium chloride has a giant ionic lattice of oppositely charged ions.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 2.  There are strong electrostatic forces of attraction between the oppositely charged ions, acting in all directions.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 3.  A large amount of energy is needed to overcome these forces, so the melting point is high.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 4.  In the solid the ions are held in fixed positions and cannot move, so there are no mobile charged particles to carry the current.\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf3\sa160\sb120\fs4 \par
\pard\sa60\sb0\keepn\fs18\cf3 Question 9  \u183?  Bonding, structure and the properties of matter  \u183?  cs-chem-metallic-bonding-4\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\keepn\fs24\b Explain, in terms of structure and bonding, why pure copper conducts electricity and can be bent into shape. [4 marks]\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs20 Marks: 4      Command word: Explain\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Mark scheme \u8212? one mark for each point\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 1.  Copper has a giant metallic structure of positive metal ions in a regular arrangement.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 2.  The outer electrons are delocalised and free to move through the whole structure.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 3.  These delocalised electrons move through the metal and carry charge, so copper conducts electricity.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 4.  The layers of ions are all the same size, so they can slide over one another without breaking the metallic bonding, which is why copper can be bent.\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf3\sa160\sb120\fs4 \par
\pard\sa60\sb0\keepn\fs18\cf3 Question 10  \u183?  The rate and extent of chemical change  \u183?  cs-chem-rates-practical-4\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\keepn\fs24\b Sodium thiosulfate solution reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid to produce a cloudy precipitate of sulfur. Describe a method a student could use to investigate how the concentration of sodium thiosulfate affects the rate of this reaction. [4 marks]\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs20 Marks: 4      Command word: Describe\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Mark scheme \u8212? one mark for each point\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 1.  Measure a fixed volume of sodium thiosulfate solution of known concentration into a conical flask, and stand the flask on a printed cross.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 2.  Add a fixed volume of dilute hydrochloric acid and start a stopwatch at the moment the acid is added.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 3.  Stop the stopwatch when the cross can no longer be seen through the solution, and record the time.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 4.  Repeat with different concentrations of sodium thiosulfate, keeping the volume of each solution, the acid concentration and the temperature the same.\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs10 \par
