\pard\sa60\sb0\keepn\fs18\cf3 Question 11  \u183?  Energy  \u183?  cs-phys-bouncing-ball-6\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\keepn\fs24\b A ball is dropped from a height of 1.0 m onto a hard floor. It bounces back to a height of 0.6 m. Describe the energy transfers that take place from the moment the ball is released until it reaches the top of its bounce, and explain why it does not return to its original height. [6 marks]\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs20 Marks: 6      Command word: Describe\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Level descriptors\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 3 (5-6 marks)}\line The full sequence is described using named stores and correct transfers: gravitational potential to kinetic on the way down, a brief store in the elastic potential of the deformed ball at the floor, kinetic on the way up, and gravitational potential at the top. The shortfall is then explained through dissipation \u8212? energy transferred to thermal stores of the ball, floor and surroundings by heating, and a little to the surroundings by sound \u8212? with the total energy stated as conserved.\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 2 (3-4 marks)}\line The main transfers are correct but a stage is missing or a store is misnamed, and the loss of height is attributed to energy being "lost" rather than dissipated to a named store. Conservation of energy may be stated without being applied.\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 1 (1-2 marks)}\line Isolated correct statements, such as "potential energy turns into kinetic energy" or "some energy is lost as heat", without an ordered account.\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Indicative content\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  As the ball falls, energy is transferred from its gravitational potential store to its kinetic store.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  The ball is moving fastest just before it hits the floor, when the kinetic store is greatest.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  On contact the ball deforms, and energy is transferred to the elastic potential store of the ball.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  As the ball regains its shape, energy is transferred back to its kinetic store and it moves upwards.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  As it rises, energy transfers from the kinetic store back to the gravitational potential store.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  Not all of the energy returns: some is dissipated to the thermal stores of the ball, the floor and the surrounding air by heating.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  A small amount is transferred to the surroundings by sound waves \u8212? the noise of the bounce.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  The total energy is conserved, but less energy is in the gravitational potential store at the top of the bounce, so the ball rises to a lower height.\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Marking points (as stored, for point-by-point marking)\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 1.  Falling: energy transfers from the gravitational potential store to the kinetic store.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 2.  The kinetic store is greatest just before impact.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 3.  On impact the ball deforms and energy transfers to its elastic potential store.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 4.  As the ball regains shape, energy transfers back to the kinetic store and it moves upward.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 5.  Rising: energy transfers from the kinetic store to the gravitational potential store.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 6.  Some energy is dissipated to thermal stores of the ball, floor and surroundings by heating, and some by sound.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 7.  Energy is conserved overall, but less is in the gravitational potential store at the top, so the bounce is lower.\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf3\sa160\sb120\fs4 \par
\pard\sa60\sb0\keepn\fs18\cf3 Question 12  \u183?  Forces  \u183?  cs-phys-terminal-velocity-6\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\keepn\fs24\b A skydiver jumps from a stationary helicopter. Explain, in terms of the forces acting on her, how her motion changes from the moment she jumps until she reaches a steady speed, and then again after she opens her parachute. [6 marks]\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs20 Marks: 6      Command word: Explain\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Level descriptors\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 3 (5-6 marks)}\line Both stages are explained through the resultant force, not just described. The initial acceleration is linked to weight being greater than air resistance; the growth of air resistance with speed is linked to a falling resultant force and therefore a falling acceleration; terminal velocity is identified as the point where the forces are balanced and the resultant force is zero. Opening the parachute is explained as a sudden increase in air resistance making the resultant force act upwards, so she decelerates, until a new, lower terminal velocity is reached.\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 2 (3-4 marks)}\line Both stages are addressed and the forces are named, but the reasoning is incomplete \u8212? for example terminal velocity described as "the forces are equal" without linking it to a zero resultant force, or the deceleration after opening the parachute described without saying that air resistance now exceeds weight.\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\li340\fs21 {\b Level 1 (1-2 marks)}\line Correct naming of weight and air resistance, or a statement that she reaches a steady speed, with no use of the resultant force.\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Indicative content\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  At the instant she jumps her speed is zero, so air resistance is zero and the only significant force is her weight.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  The resultant force is downwards and large, so she accelerates downwards at first.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  As her speed increases, the air resistance acting upwards increases.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  The resultant force therefore decreases, so her acceleration decreases \u8212? she is still speeding up, but less quickly.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  When air resistance has grown until it is equal to her weight, the resultant force is zero and she falls at a constant speed: terminal velocity.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  Opening the parachute greatly increases the surface area, so air resistance increases suddenly and is now greater than her weight.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  The resultant force now acts upwards, so she decelerates.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 \u8226?  As she slows down the air resistance falls again until it once more equals her weight, and she reaches a new, much lower terminal velocity at which she lands safely.\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Marking points (as stored, for point-by-point marking)\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 1.  At the moment she jumps, air resistance is zero and weight is the only significant force.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 2.  The resultant force is downwards, so she accelerates.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 3.  As speed increases, air resistance increases.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 4.  The resultant force decreases, so the acceleration decreases.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 5.  When air resistance equals weight the resultant force is zero and she falls at terminal velocity.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 6.  Opening the parachute increases the surface area and so increases air resistance, which is now greater than weight.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 7.  The resultant force acts upwards so she decelerates, until the forces balance again at a new, lower terminal velocity.\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf3\sa160\sb120\fs4 \par
\pard\sa60\sb0\keepn\fs18\cf3 Question 13  \u183?  Waves  \u183?  cs-phys-em-hazards-4\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\keepn\fs24\b Ultraviolet, X-rays and gamma rays are hazardous to human tissue, while radio waves are not. Explain why, and give one effect on the body of over-exposure to ultraviolet. [4 marks]\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs20 Marks: 4      Command word: Explain\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Mark scheme \u8212? one mark for each point\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 1.  Ultraviolet, X-rays and gamma rays have much higher frequencies (and shorter wavelengths) than radio waves.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 2.  A higher frequency means the waves transfer more energy to the tissue they are absorbed by.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 3.  X-rays and gamma rays are ionising: they can remove electrons from atoms and so damage the DNA in cells, which can cause mutations and cancer.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 4.  Over-exposure to ultraviolet ages the skin prematurely and increases the risk of skin cancer; it can also damage the eyes.\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf3\sa160\sb120\fs4 \par
\pard\sa60\sb0\keepn\fs18\cf3 Question 14  \u183?  Forces  \u183?  cs-phys-braking-distance-4\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\keepn\fs24\b A car travelling at 30 m/s has a much longer braking distance than the same car travelling at 15 m/s \u8212? more than twice as long. Explain why. [4 marks]\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs20 Marks: 4      Command word: Explain\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Mark scheme \u8212? one mark for each point\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 1.  Braking requires the work done by the braking force to equal the energy in the car's kinetic store.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 2.  The kinetic energy of the car is proportional to the square of its speed, so doubling the speed makes the kinetic store four times as large.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 3.  The braking force the tyres can provide is roughly the same in both cases.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 4.  Since work done equals force multiplied by distance, four times the energy needs about four times the distance to be transferred away, so the braking distance is much more than doubled.\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs10 \par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf3\sa160\sb120\fs4 \par
\pard\sa60\sb0\keepn\fs18\cf3 Question 15  \u183?  Electricity  \u183?  cs-phys-national-grid-4\par
\pard\sa120\sb0\keepn\fs24\b Electricity is transmitted across the National Grid at a very high potential difference. Explain why this makes transmission more efficient, and describe the role of the transformers at each end. [4 marks]\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs20 Marks: 4      Command word: Explain\par
\pard\sa80\sb0\keepn\fs22\b Mark scheme \u8212? one mark for each point\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 1.  For a given power transmitted, increasing the potential difference decreases the current in the cables.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 2.  The power wasted by heating the cables depends on the square of the current, so a smaller current wastes far less energy.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 3.  A step-up transformer at the power station increases the potential difference and reduces the current for transmission.\par
\pard\sa60\sb0\li340\fs21 4.  A step-down transformer near the consumer reduces the potential difference again to a much lower, safer value for use in homes.\par
\pard\sa160\sb0\fs10 \par
